---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../config';

/**
 * Written against verified behaviour, not boilerplate:
 *  - the app's only outbound requests are in internal/blocklist/blocklist.go
 *  - the browser-config changes are the DoH machine policy and the Chrome
 *    proxy-permission extension disabling
 *  - the site sets no cookies of its own; Vercel Web Analytics is cookieless
 */
---

<Base
  title="Privacy | Stick"
  description="What Stick collects, what it doesn't, and the two requests the app makes to GitHub."
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pt-16 pb-14 md:pt-24">
    <h1 class="headline text-hero">Privacy</h1>
    <div class="mt-9 space-y-5 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
      <p>
        {site.legalEntity} runs all three of these, and this covers all three: the
        Stick app, this website, and buying a Stick.
      </p>
      <p class="text-ink">
        The short version: the app sends us nothing at all. Buying one means
        Shopify handles your payment and we see your name and shipping address so
        we can post you a key. That’s the whole of it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[15px]">Last updated {site.policyUpdated}.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-5 pb-16">
    <div class="space-y-12">
      <article class="rule pt-7">
        <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          The app sends us nothing
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          There is no account, no licence check, no telemetry and no crash
          reporting. We do not know what you block, when you start a session, how
          long you lock for, or whether you ever open the app. This isn’t a
          policy we could quietly reverse. There is no server of ours for the
          app to talk to.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Your modes, your blocked sites and apps, your session history and your
          activity charts are stored on your machine and stay there.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rule pt-7">
        <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          The app does talk to GitHub, twice
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Two things are downloaded, both public files on GitHub, neither of them
          from us:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          <li class="rule pt-3">
            When you switch on a category, Explicit content or Gambling, its
            blocklist is fetched from the public StevenBlack hosts lists.
          </li>
          <li class="rule pt-3">
            The bypass-domain list, which covers encrypted-DNS, VPN and proxy
            services, refreshes from HaGeZi’s public list about once a week.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          These requests carry nothing about you beyond what any file download
          does: GitHub sees your IP address and which file you asked for. They do
          not tell GitHub, or us, which individual sites you block, because
          that list never leaves your machine. Both files are cached locally, so the
          app works offline once it has them.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          If you want to check this rather than take our word for it, run Little
          Snitch or a similar tool. Those two requests are all you’ll see.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rule pt-7">
        <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          What the app changes on your machine
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Not privacy in the data-collection sense, but you should know what it
          touches, because a background service running as root is a real thing
          to install:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          <li class="rule pt-3">
            A marked region of your system hosts file, restored within two
            seconds if it’s edited during a session. Entries outside that region
            are never touched.
          </li>
          <li class="rule pt-3">A firewall ruleset while a session is running.</li>
          <li class="rule pt-3">
            Browser configuration. Encrypted DNS is switched off by machine
            policy, and Chrome extensions that request proxy permissions are
            disabled during a session. Password managers and ad blockers are left
            alone. This policy persists across restarts, because browsers only
            read it when they launch.
          </li>
          <li class="rule pt-3">
            Running applications you’ve chosen to block are quit if you open
            them.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <article class="rule pt-7">
        <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          Buying one
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Checkout is handled entirely by Shopify on Shopify’s own page. They
          collect your name, email address, shipping address and payment details,
          and their privacy policy governs what happens there.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          What we can see afterwards is your name, email address, shipping
          address, what you ordered and whether it paid. <strong
            class="font-semibold text-ink">We never see your full card number</strong
          >. Shopify shows us the last four digits and the card type, nothing more.
          We use this to post you a key and to answer you if you write in.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Your address is shared with the postal or courier service that carries
          the parcel, because that is how parcels work.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rule pt-7">
        <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          This website
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          <strong class="font-semibold text-ink">We set no cookies.</strong> There
          is no consent banner on this site because there is nothing to consent to.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The site is hosted on Vercel, which keeps standard server logs
          including IP addresses. We use Vercel Web Analytics, which is
          cookieless: it records the page visited, the referring site, rough
          country, and browser and device type, in aggregate. It does not set a
          persistent identifier, does not follow you across other websites, and
          cannot tell us who you are.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rule pt-7">
        <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          Email
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          If you write to{' '}
          <a
            href={`mailto:${site.supportEmail}`}
            class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4">{site.supportEmail}</a
          >, we keep the message so we can help you and remember what happened
          last time. That inbox is hosted by Microsoft Outlook, so Microsoft
          processes it as any mail provider does.
        </p>
        {
          site.buttondownUser ? (
            <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
              If you give us your email address — to hear when Stick ships, or
              for occasional updates — it is stored with
              Buttondown and used for nothing else. We record which of those you
              asked about so we don't send you the other ones. No account, no
              password: an address in a box is the whole of it. Every message has
              an unsubscribe link, and unsubscribing deletes you from the list.
            </p>
          ) : (
            <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
              We don’t run a mailing list.
            </p>
          )
        }
      </article>

      <article class="rule pt-7">
        <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          What we never do
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          We don’t sell your data, rent it, or share it for advertising. There
          are no advertising or social tracking pixels on this site. We don’t
          build profiles, and we couldn’t build one from app usage even if we
          wanted to, because none of it reaches us.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rule pt-7">
        <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          How long we keep things
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Order records are kept as long as tax and accounting rules require,
          which in most places is several years. We can’t delete those on
          request while that obligation stands. Support emails are kept while
          they’re useful and deleted when they aren’t. Analytics data is
          aggregated and holds nothing personal to delete.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rule pt-7">
        <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          Your rights
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Wherever you are, you can ask us what we hold about you, ask for a
          copy, ask us to correct it, or ask us to delete it. If you’re in the
          UK, EU, California or somewhere with similar law, those rights are
          yours by statute; we’ll honour them regardless of where you live.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Email{' '}
          <a
            href={`mailto:${site.supportEmail}`}
            class="text-accent underline underline-offset-4">{site.supportEmail}</a
          > and we’ll answer {site.unlockResponseTime}. There’s very little to
          hand over: an order record, and possibly an email thread.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rule pt-7">
        <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          Children
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Stick isn’t aimed at children and we don’t knowingly collect anything
          from anyone under 13. It’s a tool for people choosing to limit their
          own computer, not for monitoring somebody else’s.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="rule pt-7">
        <h2 class="text-[22px] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em]">
          Changes
        </h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-relaxed text-muted">
          If this changes in a way that matters, we’ll date it here and email
          anyone who has bought a Stick. We’re not going to quietly start
          collecting things. The claim that the app talks to nothing of ours is
          load-bearing, and we’d rather lose the argument than the trust.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
